            {
              "id": "2c910491-3474-376e-9081-661bc14a6fb3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9688fb72-879e-377f-adbe-4d8219287877",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to but not including the first null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character (`'\\0'`) at the end, or by using functions that automatically null-terminate the string for you, such as `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen`, consider using `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum length of the string. This can prevent buffer overflows by ensuring that `strnlen` does not read past the end of the buffer.\n\nHere is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strnlen\n\n// ...\n\nconst size_t max_length = 100; // replace with the actual maximum length\ndetail::widen_impl(str, std::strnlen(str, max_length));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides functions to manipulate C strings and arrays.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "913104a6-c0e2-32f2-ba50-ceb365778aa1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or input validation, which can lead to serious security issues.\n\nIn the provided code, `std::wcslen(str)` is a function that can lead to buffer overflow if the string `str` is not null-terminated. This function calculates the length of a wide string until it encounters a null character. If a null character is not found, the function may continue reading beyond the memory allocated for the string, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or input validation. In this case, you can use `std::wstring` and its `length()` or `size()` member functions to get the length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::wcslen(str)` function with `std::wstring::length()` or `std::wstring::size()` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\nstd::wstring wstr = str;\ndetail::narrow_impl(wstr, wstr.length());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This is required for `std::wstring`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "79ab5b78-9e7f-3749-b932-ab53782ce9aa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. One such function is `mbsrtowcs`.\n\nThe `mbsrtowcs` function is used to convert a multibyte string to a wide character string. However, it does not perform any bounds checking on the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is too large.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `mbsrtowcs` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking, such as `mbsrtowcs_s` (in Microsoft's C library) or `mbsnrtowcs` (in GNU's C library).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `mbsrtowcs` function with `mbsnrtowcs` or `mbsrtowcs_s` and ensure to provide the size of the destination buffer as an argument to these functions.\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe code\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsrtowcs(dest, &src, 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (GNU)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nmbsnrtowcs(dest, &src, strlen(src), 100, NULL);\n\n// Safe code (Microsoft)\nchar* src = /* source multibyte string */;\nwchar_t dest[100];\nsize_t converted;\nmbsrtowcs_s(&converted, dest, 100, &src, _TRUNCATE, NULL);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library (`<cstdlib>` in C++, `<stdlib.h>` in C) for the `mbsrtowcs`, `mbsnrtowcs`, and `mbsrtowcs_s` functions, and the string library (`<cstring>` in C++, `<string.h>` in C) for the `strlen` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
